The main difference is how conflicts are handled. The principal difference between interface extension and intersections is how conflicts are handled, and that is typically one of the main reasons to pick one over the other.

**Interfaces:** If interfaces are defined with the same name, TypeScript will attempt to merge them if the properties are compatible. If the properties are not compatible (same property name but different types), TypeScript raises an error.

```ts
interface Person {
  name: string;
}

interface Person {
  name: number;
}
// error: incompatible properties
```

**Intersections:** Properties with different types are merged automatically. When the type is used later, TypeScript expects the property to satisfy both types simultaneously, which may produce unexpected results. The code compiles, but `Staff` would require `name` to be both a string and a number, so the property is of type `never`.

```ts
type Staff = Person1 & Person2  // name: string & number → never
```

There is also a related case for discriminant properties. Since TypeScript 3.9, if an intersection is impossible because of incompatible discriminant properties (like `kind: "circle"` and `kind: "square"`), the whole intersection type collapses to `never`, rather than just the property.

