answers.text:
`Omit<Type, Keys>` constructs a type by picking all properties from `Type` and then removing `Keys` (a string literal or union of string literals). It is the opposite of `Pick`. It was released in TypeScript 3.5.

Example:


```ts
type Person = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  location: string;
};

type QuantumPerson = Omit<Person, "location">;

// equivalent to
type QuantumPerson = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};
```


You can omit several keys at once with a union, e.g. `Omit<Todo, "completed" | "createdAt">`.

The 2.8 release notes also say `Omit<T, K>` is trivially written as `Pick<T, Exclude<keyof T, K>>`.

