`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` adds `undefined` to a type when it is accessed using an index. It was introduced in TypeScript 4.1. Under this mode, every property access (like `foo.bar`) or indexed access (like `foo["bar"]`) is considered potentially undefined. In practice, turning it on adds `undefined` to any un-declared field in the type.

**Example effect:** With an index signature of `string | number`, `opts.yadda` will have the type `string | number | undefined` instead of just `string | number`. To access it, you'll either have to check for its existence first or use a non-null assertion operator (the postfix `!` character).

```ts
if (opts.yadda) {
  console.log(opts.yadda.toString());
}

opts.yadda!.toString();
```

**Arrays:** Indexing into an array is also more strictly checked, even in a bounds-checked loop. A `for (let i...)` loop with `strs[i].toUpperCase()` will error. If you don't need the indexes, you can iterate over individual elements by using a `for`-`of` loop or a `forEach` call.

**Not part of `strict`:** The flag can be handy for catching out-of-bounds errors, but it might be noisy for a lot of code, so it is not automatically enabled by the `strict` flag.

