answers.text:
The search results don't define TS7006 directly, but they show it in context. Several examples are annotated with `// @errors: 7006`, which corresponds to the "implicit any" situation described below.

- When no type annotations are present, TypeScript falls back to `any` for a variable when it cannot infer the type. With `noImplicitAny` on, TypeScript issues an error whenever it would have inferred `any`. The example for this option is annotated with error 7006:

```ts
// @errors: 7006
function fn(s) {
  console.log(s.subtr(3));
}
```

Here the parameter `s` has no type annotation, so it implicitly has type `any`.

- The classes documentation shows that an `implements` clause doesn't fix this. `implements` clauses don't change how the class body is checked or its type inferred, so an unannotated parameter like `s` in `check(s)` is still implicitly `any`. That example is also annotated with 7006.

The results don't give the official error message text. Based on the examples, though, TS7006 appears to be the error raised when a parameter has no type annotation and its type can't be inferred, under `noImplicitAny`. The usual fix is to add an explicit type annotation.

