The search results don't define TS7006 directly, but they show it in context. Several examples are annotated with `@errors: 7006`, which corresponds to this error code.

In those examples, the error appears where a parameter has no type annotation and TypeScript can't infer its type. In some cases where no type annotations are present, TypeScript will fall back to a type of `any` for a variable when it cannot infer the type. With the `noImplicitAny` option on, TypeScript will issue an error whenever it would have inferred `any`. The example in the docs is:

```ts
// @errors: 7006
function fn(s) {
  console.log(s.subtr(3));
}
```

Here the parameter `s` has no type, so it is implicitly `any`.

The results also show that an `implements` clause won't fix this. `implements` clauses don't change how the class body is checked or its type inferred, so an unannotated parameter like `s` in `check(s)` still triggers 7006.

The results never spell out the error's message text, so this explanation is inferred from the examples. The fix they imply is to add an explicit type annotation to the parameter.

