Error TS7006 means "Parameter 's' implicitly has an 'any' type." It is reported when TypeScript can't infer a type and no annotation is present. In some cases where no type annotations are present, TypeScript will fall back to a type of `any` for a variable when it cannot infer the type. With `noImplicitAny` turned on, TypeScript issues an error whenever it would have inferred `any`.

Example from the docs:

```ts
function fn(s) {
  console.log(s.subtr(3));
}
```

This produces TS7006 on `function fn(s) {`.

A related case: an `implements` clause doesn't change how the class body is checked or its types inferred, so a method parameter like `check(s)` in a class implementing an interface still gets TS7006, even if the interface declares `name: string`.

The fix is to add an explicit type annotation to the parameter. The search results imply this but don't show it directly.

